-- #1: Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya (Mumbai, Maharashtra)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya, formerly the Prince of Wales Museum, is Mumbai''s premier institution for art, archaeology, and natural history. Founded in the early twentieth century to commemorate the royal visit of the Prince of Wales, the museum is housed in a magnificent Indo-Saracenic building designed by architect George Wittet, set amidst landscaped heritage gardens. Its permanent collection comprises over fifty thousand artefacts spanning ancient Indian civilizations, medieval empires, and international cultures. Visitors can explore exceptional galleries featuring Indus Valley terracotta, Gandharan Buddhist sculptures, intricately carved ivory, miniature paintings from Mughal and Rajasthani schools, European oil masterworks, and a remarkable arms and armour gallery. Recognized internationally for its architectural conservation and cultural programs, the museum provides an indispensable window into the artistic, historical, and aesthetic traditions of the Indian subcontinent.'
WHERE id = 1;

-- #2: Nehru Science Centre (Mumbai, Maharashtra)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Nehru Science Centre in Worli, Mumbai, is India''s largest interactive science museum and a cornerstone institution of the National Council of Science Museums under the Ministry of Culture. Spanning an expansive landscaped campus, the centre is designed to make scientific discovery engaging and accessible through more than five hundred hands-on exhibits across thematic indoor galleries. Visitors can experiment with interactive installations covering sound and hearing, human biology, optics, nuclear power, mechanics, and renewable energy. The sprawling outdoor science park integrates plants, energy models, and physical science demonstrations into natural open spaces. The centre also features a high-definition 3D science cinema, interactive demonstration corners, and regular sky observation programs that engage students and families alike. By transforming complex scientific principles into tangible, participatory experiences, the centre plays a pivotal role in promoting scientific literacy and technological curiosity across Western India.'
WHERE id = 2;

-- #3: Raja Dinkar Kelkar Museum (Mumbai, Maharashtra)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Raja Dinkar Kelkar Museum preserves one of India''s most extraordinary private collections of traditional craftsmanship and everyday decorative arts. Created through the lifelong dedication of Dr. D. G. Kelkar in memory of his son Dinkar, the museum holds more than twenty thousand historic artefacts primarily from the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. The exhibits illuminate the daily aesthetic life, domestic culture, and artistic ingenuity of historical Maharashtra and neighboring regions. Highlights include the Mastani Mahal replica, elaborately carved wooden palace doors, brass oil lamps, ornate betel nut cutters, musical instruments, textiles, kitchen utensils, hookahs, and traditional Maharashtrian footwear. The collection reflects the deep artistic sensitivity embedded in regional household items, religious rituals, and Maratha architectural design, offering visitors an intimate and authentic look into the cultural heritage and traditional artisanal legacy of the region.'
WHERE id = 3;

-- #4: Dr. Bhau Daji Lad Museum (Mumbai, Maharashtra)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Dr. Bhau Daji Lad Museum, established in 1872 as the Victoria and Albert Museum, is Mumbai''s oldest museum and a landmark of nineteenth-century civic architecture. Located in Byculla, the museum is housed in an exquisitely restored High Victorian Neo-Renaissance building featuring Corinthian columns, etched ceiling gilding, and imported Minton encaustic floor tiles. The museum showcases the cultural history, industrial arts, and diverse communities of nineteenth-century Bombay through an invaluable collection of miniature clay models, dioramas, silver filigree, Bidri ware, lacquer work, and carved wood. Rare cartographic displays and archival photographs document the gradual urban transformation of Bombay from a cluster of fishing islands into a bustling commercial metropolis. Honored with a UNESCO Asia-Pacific Heritage Award for its meticulous architectural restoration, the museum serves as a vital cultural repository celebrating Mumbai''s multifaceted urban and artisanal heritage.'
WHERE id = 4;

-- #5: National Museum (New Delhi, Delhi)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The National Museum, situated on Janpath in the heart of New Delhi, is India''s premier cultural institution and custodian of over two hundred thousand artworks spanning five millennia. Established in 1949 under the Ministry of Culture, the museum presents a panoramic journey through Indian civilization, from prehistoric eras to modern times. Among its most celebrated treasures are the iconic bronze Dancing Girl and terracotta figurines from the Indus Valley Civilization, sacred Buddhist relics excavated from Piprahwa, magnificent Mauryan and Gupta stone sculptures, and Chola bronzes. The museum also houses rare Central Asian silks and wall paintings collected by Sir Aurel Stein, Mughal and Rajasthani miniature paintings, traditional costumes, decorative arts, and an extensive collection of historical weaponry. As the nation''s leading museum, it serves as a central hub for archaeological scholarship, conservation, and public cultural education.'
WHERE id = 5;

-- #6: National Gallery of Modern Art (New Delhi, Delhi)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The National Gallery of Modern Art in New Delhi is the country''s flagship institution dedicated to modern and contemporary Indian visual arts. Housed in the historic Jaipur House near India Gate, the gallery opened in 1954 under the Ministry of Culture to preserve, showcase, and foster the evolving artistic traditions of India since the mid-nineteenth century. Its permanent collection contains more than seventeen thousand artworks, representing the visionary transition from traditional court art to modern abstraction. Visitors can view landmark paintings by pioneers such as Raja Ravi Varma, Amrita Sher-Gil, Rabindranath Tagore, Jamini Roy, and Nandalal Bose, alongside seminal works by Progressive Artists'' Group masters including M. F. Husain, F. N. Souza, and S. H. Raza. With dynamic retrospective exhibitions, specialized art libraries, and a landscaped sculpture garden, the gallery stands as an essential center for Indian artistic modernism.'
WHERE id = 6;

-- #7: Gandhi Smriti (New Delhi, Delhi)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'Gandhi Smriti, situated at Old Birla House on Tees January Marg in New Delhi, is a revered national memorial preserving the sacred ground where Mahatma Gandhi spent the final 144 days of his life. It was here that he conducted evening interfaith prayer meetings and where he was assassinated on January 30, 1948. The memorial preserves the unpretentious room where Gandhiji lived, maintaining his modest personal effects including his walking stick, spectacles, sandals, and spinning wheel. A stone pathway marked with footsteps traces his final walk toward the prayer grounds, culminating at the Martyr''s Column. Within the building, the ''Eternal Gandhi'' multimedia exhibition utilizes interactive digital installations to convey his philosophies of Satyagraha, non-violence, truth, and peace. Gandhi Smriti remains a profoundly moving site of national reflection and global pilgrimage for champions of universal harmony.'
WHERE id = 7;

-- #8: Government Museum, Chennai (Chennai, Tamil Nadu)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Government Museum at Egmore, Chennai, established in 1851, is the second oldest museum in India and an architectural jewel of the city. Spread over a historic colonial campus, the complex includes Indo-Saracenic heritage buildings housing comprehensive galleries of archaeology, geology, zoology, numismatics, and anthropology. The museum is globally renowned for its exceptional South Indian Bronze Gallery, which displays priceless Chola-period masterworks including the world-famous bronze Nataraja, Shiva Ardhanarishvara, and Parvati idols. Another major highlight is the Amaravati Gallery, preserving exquisite limestone Buddhist relief panels and carved stupa railings dating from the second century BCE. The museum also showcases prehistoric paleolithic implements, Roman coins recovered from ancient Tamil trading ports, and folk cultural artefacts. Together with the neighboring historic Connemara Public Library and Museum Theatre, it serves as South India''s foremost treasure trove of antiquities.'
WHERE id = 8;

-- #9: Vivekananda House (Chennai, Tamil Nadu)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'Vivekananda House, historically known as Ice House or Vivekanandar Illam, stands prominently along Marina Beach in Chennai as a cherished spiritual and historic landmark. Originally built in 1842 by Frederic Tudor as an innovative warehouse for storing natural ice imported from New England, the building gained enduring national significance in February 1897 when Swami Vivekananda stayed here for nine days following his triumphant address at the Parliament of Religions in Chicago. Today maintained by the Ramakrishna Math, the museum features inspirational multimedia exhibits, a serene meditation room, photographic archives, and a stereoscopic 3D presentation tracing Swamiji''s life, teachings, and spiritual philosophy. Visitors can explore displays highlighting India''s ancient cultural heritage and Swamiji''s patriotic calls for social upliftment. The site remains a vibrant pilgrimage center inspiring visitors with values of universal harmony and selfless service.'
WHERE id = 9;

-- #10: The Periyar Science and Technology Museum (Chennai, Tamil Nadu)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Periyar Science and Technology Museum, located at Kotturpuram in Chennai, was established in 1983 by the Tamil Nadu Science and Technology Centre to popularize scientific inquiry among the public. Named in honor of social reformer Thanthai Periyar, the institution is renowned for hosting the B. M. Birla Planetarium, featuring a sophisticated full-dome digital sky projection system that offers immersive astronomical shows. The museum''s interactive galleries cover physical sciences, transport engineering, electronics and communications, energy conservation, life sciences, and defense technologies. Hands-on participatory models allow children and visitors to directly test physical principles, explore mechanics, and learn about satellite telemetry. The surrounding grounds include a science park, traffic park, and energy park that combine outdoor recreation with experiential education, making it an essential educational destination for school groups and families across Tamil Nadu.'
WHERE id = 10;

-- #11: Kamaraj Memorial House (Chennai, Tamil Nadu)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'Kamaraj Memorial House, located on Thirumalai Pillai Road in T. Nagar, Chennai, honors the memory of Bharat Ratna awardee K. Kamaraj, freedom fighter and revered former Chief Minister of Tamil Nadu. The humble two-storey residence where Kamaraj lived and passed away in 1975 has been preserved as a biographical museum that captures his legendary simplicity, integrity, and statesmanship. The ground floor displays his modest living quarters, personal khadi clothing, simple wooden cot, spectacles, and extensive collection of books on political economy and world history. The upper floor exhibits historical photographs, commemorative plaques, state awards, and diplomatic gifts received during his international tours. Informational panels document his visionary initiatives, particularly the pioneering free midday meal scheme that revolutionized school education in Tamil Nadu. The memorial offers an inspiring testament to selfless public leadership.'
WHERE id = 11;

-- #12: Indian Museum (Kolkata, West Bengal)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Indian Museum in Kolkata, founded in 1814 under the guidance of Danish botanist Dr. Nathaniel Wallich and the Asiatic Society of Bengal, is the oldest and largest multipurpose museum in India. Housed in a grand Neoclassical building on Jawaharlal Nehru Road, the museum features thirty-five galleries organized into six sections: Art, Archaeology, Anthropology, Geology, Zoology, and Economic Botany. Its collection includes invaluable historical treasures such as the second-century BCE sculpted Buddhist gateway from Bharhut, rare Ashokan stone edicts, an authentic four-thousand-year-old Egyptian mummy, dinosaur and mammalian fossils, and meteorite fragments. Its art galleries house miniature paintings, Tibetan thangkas, Persian calligraphy, and regional decorative crafts. Locally affectionately known as ''Jadu Ghar'' or the Wonder House, the Indian Museum remains an internationally celebrated institution chronicling the natural history, material culture, and artistic legacy of South Asia.'
WHERE id = 12;

-- #13: Birla Industrial & Technological Museum (Kolkata, West Bengal)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Birla Industrial & Technological Museum, located on Gurusaday Road in Kolkata, opened in 1959 as India''s pioneering interactive science museum. Established under the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research and now part of the National Council of Science Museums, the institution revolutionized museum education by introducing working models and participatory science demonstrations. The museum''s most famous attraction is a life-sized simulated underground coal mine that guides visitors through the equipment, dangers, and engineering behind deep mining. Other permanent galleries showcase transport systems, motive power, electricity and magnetism, biotechnology, television technology, and mathematics. Daily science shows, planetarium sessions, and astronomy workshops provide hands-on learning for thousands of students each year. As the birthplace of India''s science centre movement, the museum continues to inspire curiosity and inventive thinking in young minds across Eastern India.'
WHERE id = 13;

-- #14: Netaji Bhawan (Kolkata, West Bengal)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'Netaji Bhawan, situated on Elgin Road in Kolkata, is the historic ancestral residence of Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose and a revered memorial to India''s freedom struggle. Maintained by the Netaji Research Bureau, the building served as the nerve centre of Netaji''s political activities and was the site of his dramatic Great Escape under British house arrest in January 1941. Visitors can view his preserved bedroom, study, and the historic 1937 Wanderer car in which he was driven away to begin his clandestine journey across Asia and Europe. The museum displays personal belongings, military uniforms of the Indian National Army, historical documents, letters exchanged with Mahatma Gandhi and Rabindranath Tagore, battle maps, and recordings of his rousing wartime speeches. Netaji Bhawan stands as an evocative monument honoring one of the most charismatic and resolute leaders of the Indian independence movement.'
