-- #17: Government Museum, Bengaluru (Bengaluru, Karnataka)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Government Museum in Bengaluru, established in 1865 in the leafy grounds of Cubbon Park, is one of the oldest archaeological museums in South India. Housed in a handsome red Neoclassical building designed by Colonel Richard Sankey, the museum preserves an invaluable assemblage of South Indian antiquities, epigraphy, and fine arts. Its most historic exhibit is the ninth-century Begur stone inscription, celebrated as the earliest known epigraphical record explicitly referencing the name ''Bengaluru''. Visitors can examine exquisite stone sculptures from the Hoysala, Nolamba, and Ganga dynasties, terracotta figurines from Chandravalli, Vijayanagara coins, ancient weaponry, and traditional Tanjore and Mysore paintings. Adjoining the Venkatappa Art Gallery, this venerable institution offers an enriching historical exploration of Karnataka''s political history, temple architecture, and sculptural mastery.'
