-- #28: Amaravati Archaeological Museum (Amaravati, Andhra Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Amaravati Archaeological Museum, situated in the ancient village of Amaravati along the banks of the Krishna River, is a dedicated site museum maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India. The museum preserves the invaluable sculptural treasures retrieved from the nearby Mahachaitya, or Great Stupa, one of the most magnificent Buddhist monuments of the ancient world, dating from the third century BCE to the third century CE. Housed across thematic galleries, the museum displays limestone relief medallions, carved drum slabs, and torana gateway fragments showing the distinctive Amaravati school of art. Sculptural reliefs depict key events from the life of Gautama Buddha and scenes from the Jataka tales with dynamic narrative elegance. Also on display are Satavahana and Ikshvaku coins, crystal reliquaries, and inscriptions, making the museum a globally significant destination for Buddhist art and epigraphical study.'
