-- #35: Srimanta Sankardeva Sangrahalaya (Guwahati, Assam)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Srimanta Sankardeva Sangrahalaya is the centerpiece museum of the sprawling Srimanta Sankaradeva Kalakshetra cultural complex in Panjabari, Guwahati. Dedicated to the enduring legacy of the fifteenth-century saint, philosopher, playwright, and social reformer Srimanta Sankardeva, the museum serves as a vibrant repository of Assamese Vaishnavite culture and regional folklore. The museum''s galleries display sacred manuscript paintings, traditional musical instruments including the Khol and Taal, expressive Bhaona theatrical masks, brass and bell-metal liturgical utensils, and exquisite handwoven Muga and Eri silks. Life-sized dioramas depict Sattriya monastic dance rituals and rural community life, while surrounding galleries celebrate the arts and traditions of the indigenous ethnic groups of Assam. The institution plays a pivotal role in preserving and propagating the inclusive humanist philosophy and artistic brilliance of Sankardeva across generations.'
WHERE id = 35;

-- #36: Kaziranga Forest Museum (Guwahati, Assam)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Kaziranga Forest Museum, located in the vicinity of Kaziranga National Park in Assam, serves as an educational and natural history interpretation centre dedicated to the conservation of the Brahmaputra floodplains. As the guardian of the world''s largest population of the endangered Great Indian One-horned Rhinoceros, Kaziranga''s ecosystem is celebrated through detailed educational exhibits, preserved specimens, and wildlife dioramas. Visitors can examine informative displays on the park''s rich biodiversity, including wild water buffalo, Asian elephants, royal Bengal tigers, and migratory wetland birds. The museum also chronicles the inspiring history of wildlife conservation in Assam, honoring forest guards and detailing anti-poaching measures, habitat management, and ecological research. By fostering environmental awareness among travelers and local communities, the museum emphasizes the crucial importance of preserving fragile forest and wetland habitats.'
WHERE id = 36;

-- #37: Patna Museum (Patna, Bihar)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Patna Museum, locally known as Jadu Ghar, was established in 1917 during the British Raj to house the extraordinary archaeological antiquities excavated across Bihar. Constructed in an imposing Mughal-Rajput heritage style, the museum is internationally famous for housing the Didarganj Yakshi, a breathtaking life-sized polished sandstone sculpture dating to the Mauryan period in the third century BCE. Other monumental highlights include a sacred soapstone casket containing the authentic corporeal relics of Gautama Buddha excavated from Vaishali, an extensive collection of Pala-period bronzes from Kurkihar, medieval stone sculptures, and Tibetan Thangka scroll paintings brought to India by Rahul Sankrityayan. A remarkable two-hundred-million-year-old fossilized tree trunk stretching over sixteen meters adorns the museum''s courtyard, making it a monumental pilgrimage site for lovers of classical Indian sculpture and ancient history.'
WHERE id = 37;

-- #38: Bihar Museum (Patna, Bihar)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Bihar Museum, spanning an expansive 13.5-acre campus on Bailey Road in Patna, is one of the most technologically advanced and architecturally striking museum complexes in South Asia. Designed by Pritzker Prize-winning architect Fumihiko Maki and opened in 2015, the museum is dedicated to narrating the foundational role of ancient Bihar in shaping Indian civilization. The experiential galleries take visitors through chronological eras, featuring immersive exhibits on the Magadha, Maurya, and Gupta empires, the intellectual heritage of Nalanda and Vikramshila, and the life of Mahavira and the Buddha. The museum showcases regional folk and tribal arts, vibrant Madhubani painting traditions, metal crafts, and contemporary installations. With an interactive Children''s Gallery and world-class conservation facilities, the Bihar Museum has set a modern benchmark for museum curation and visitor engagement across India.'
WHERE id = 38;

-- #39: Nalanda Archaeological Museum (Patna, Bihar)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Nalanda Archaeological Museum, situated opposite the excavated ruins of the ancient Nalanda Mahavihara (a UNESCO World Heritage Site) in Bihar, is a premier site museum maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India. Established in 1917, the museum houses the priceless antiquities recovered during archaeological excavations of the renowned fifth-century monastic university. Across its four main galleries, visitors can view magnificent Pala and Gupta stone sculptures, inscribed bronze images of the Buddha and Bodhisattvas, copper plates, terracotta seals bearing the university''s official insignia, coins, and everyday pottery used by ancient Buddhist monks. The sculptures demonstrate the supreme artistry and metaphysical depth of the Nalanda bronze-casting tradition. The museum offers visitors an invaluable scholarly perspective on the intellectual, spiritual, and artistic glory of ancient India''s greatest international centre of learning.'
WHERE id = 39;

-- #40: Mahant Ghasidas Museum (Raipur, Chhattisgarh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mahant Ghasidas Memorial Museum in Raipur is one of the ten oldest museums in India, originally established in 1875 by Raja Mahant Ghasidas of the princely state of Rajnandgaon. Reorganized in an impressive modern building in 1953, the museum serves as the chief custodian of the archaeological and cultural heritage of Chhattisgarh. The museum is renowned for its splendid archaeological collection, which includes ancient stone sculptures dating from the fifth to fourteenth centuries, primarily from the historic sites of Sirpur, Malhar, and Rajim. Masterpieces include exquisite Buddhist, Jain, and Hindu carvings from the Kalachuri and Somavamsi dynasties, ancient copper-plate charters, medieval coins, and weapons. The ethnographic section displays traditional crafts, tribal clothing, and agricultural implements of regional tribes, offering visitors a comprehensive understanding of the state''s ancient and living traditions.'
WHERE id = 40;

-- #41: Tribal Museum Raipur (Raipur, Chhattisgarh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Tribal Museum in Raipur, established under the Tribal Research and Training Institute of the Government of Chhattisgarh, is dedicated to documenting and showcasing the rich cultural legacy of the state''s indigenous communities. The museum brings to life the vibrant lifestyles, beliefs, and artistic traditions of major tribal groups such as the Gond, Baiga, Maria, Muria, and Halba of the Bastar and Surguja regions. The galleries display authentic specimens of traditional bell-metal craft (Dokra), wrought-iron art (Loha Shilp), terracotta pottery, and intricate woodcarvings used in tribal shrines. Visitors can examine traditional attire, cowrie-shell ornaments, hunting and fishing gear, sacred musical instruments, and full-scale replicas of tribal dwelling huts. The museum provides an essential cultural bridge celebrating the sustainable ecological wisdom and artistic genius of Chhattisgarh''s tribal peoples.'
WHERE id = 41;

-- #42: Archaeological Museum and Portrait Gallery (Old Goa, Goa)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Archaeological Museum and Portrait Gallery, situated in the historic precinct of Old Goa, is housed in the convent adjoining the celebrated Church of St. Francis of Assisi. Maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India, the museum presents a comprehensive visual narrative of Goa''s history from prehistoric eras through the Kadamba dynasty and the four centuries of Portuguese colonial rule. The ground floor displays prehistoric stone implements, hero stones (Viragals), medieval Hindu temple sculptures, and stone inscriptions in Sanskrit, Kannada, and Persian. The upper gallery features a remarkable collection of portraits of Portuguese viceroys and governors of Goa, dating from the sixteenth to twentieth centuries. Visitors can also view vintage coins, arms, wooden furniture, and Christian religious sculptures, making it an essential destination for understanding Goa''s unique fusion of Indian and European heritage.'
WHERE id = 42;

-- #43: Naval Aviation Museum (Vasco da Gama, Goa)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Naval Aviation Museum, established in 1998 on a scenic ridge overlooking Bogmalo Beach near Vasco da Gama, Goa, is one of only two military naval aviation museums in Asia. Dedicated to preserving the valiant history of the Indian Naval Air Arm, the museum features both outdoor aircraft parks and indoor historical galleries. The outdoor park exhibits thirteen decommissioned naval aircraft, including the legendary Sea Harrier vertical-takeoff jet, the Breguet Alizé anti-submarine aircraft, the Lockheed Super Constellation, and vintage Sea King helicopters. The indoor galleries display naval weapons, torpedoes, sea mines, depth charges, aircraft sensors, historic flight gear, and commemorative battle records documenting pivotal naval operations including the 1971 Liberation of Bangladesh and Operation Vijay. Interactive flight simulators and detailed aircraft models offer visitors an inspiring, patriotic tribute to Indian naval aviators.'
WHERE id = 43;

-- #44: State Museum (Imphal, Manipur)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Manipur State Museum, established in 1969 near the historic Kangla Fort in Imphal, is the premier cultural institution dedicated to preserving the distinctive heritage, royal history, and tribal traditions of Manipur. The museum is especially famous for housing the royal boat ''Hiyang Hiren'', an impressive 78-foot carved traditional canoe with mythic dragon heads housed in an open-air gallery. Across its indoor galleries, the museum presents archaeology, ethnology, natural history, and painting. Visitors can view traditional Meitei royal costumes, weaponry, musical instruments, and the origins of modern polo through displays on Sagol Kangjei (traditional Manipuri polo). Ethnographic galleries feature traditional handloom textiles, cane ornaments, and ceremonial tools of the various Naga and Kuki-Zomi tribes residing in the surrounding hill districts, providing a panoramic understanding of Manipur''s cultural mosaic.'
WHERE id = 44;

-- #45: Gandhi Memorial Museum (Ahmedabad, Gujarat)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Gandhi Memorial Museum at the Sabarmati Ashram in Ahmedabad stands as one of the most sanctified and historic institutions of India''s independence movement. Established on the peaceful western banks of the Sabarmati River, this was the primary residence of Mahatma Gandhi and Kasturba Gandhi from 1917 to 1930, and the historic starting point of the 1930 Dandi Salt March. The museum building, designed by celebrated modernist architect Charles Correa, embodies Gandhian principles of simplicity, natural light, and serene harmony. The museum preserves Hriday Kunj, Gandhi''s modest cottage, alongside his personal spinning wheels, walking stick, letters, and rare archival photographs. The ''My Life is My Message'' gallery documents his leadership of the freedom struggle through non-violent resistance, offering millions of global visitors profound inspiration on truth, simplicity, and moral courage.'
WHERE id = 45;

-- #46: Baroda Museum (Ahmedabad, Gujarat)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Baroda Museum and Picture Gallery, founded in 1887 by the visionary Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad III in Vadodara (historically linked with regional Gujarati collections), is one of the most prominent multidisciplinary museums in Western India. Inspired by London''s Victoria and Albert Museum, the heritage building blends Victorian architectural elements with traditional Indo-Saracenic and Maratha motifs. The museum houses an astonishingly diverse and encyclopedic collection spanning art, archaeology, ethnology, natural history, and European painting. Masterpieces include an authentic Egyptian mummy, the massive 72-foot skeleton of a blue whale, Akota bronzes dating from the fifth century, Roman glass, Mughal miniatures, and rare Japanese and Chinese porcelain. The adjoining Picture Gallery exhibits seminal European oil paintings by masters such as Paolo Veronese, Turner, and Constable, reflecting the cosmopolitan cultural vision of the Gaekwad rulers.'
WHERE id = 46;

-- #47: Kurukshetra Panorama Museum (Kurukshetra, Haryana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Kurukshetra Panorama and Science Centre, situated near the sacred Brahma Sarovar in Kurukshetra, Haryana, is a unique cultural institution combining ancient epic heritage with interactive modern science. Jointly developed by the National Council of Science Museums and the Kurukshetra Development Board, the centre''s main attraction is a breathtaking 360-degree cylindrical panorama painting depicting the colossal eighteen-day battle of the Mahabharata. Accompanied by life-sized realistic dioramas, battle sound effects, and dramatic lighting, the panorama transports visitors onto the historic battlefield of Kurukshetra. The ground-floor science galleries showcase interactive exhibits on physics, mathematics, and ancient Indian scientific discoveries, explaining the principles of optics, simple machines, and sound. By seamlessly bridging ancient civilizational literature with contemporary scientific inquiry, the museum offers an immersive and educational experience for learners of all ages.'
WHERE id = 47;

-- #48: Dharohar Museum (Kurukshetra, Haryana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Dharohar Museum, established by Kurukshetra University in Haryana, is an acclaimed ethnographic institution created to preserve, showcase, and celebrate the rich folk traditions, agrarian heritage, and rural lifestyle of Haryana. Spanning over two dozen curated thematic sections, the museum recreates the vibrant architectural and domestic environment of a traditional Haryanvi village. Visitors can explore fully reconstructed village homes, agricultural tools, traditional bullock carts, brass household utensils, oil-extracting mills, and weaving looms. The textiles gallery features exquisite specimens of Phulkari embroidery, traditional ghagras, and hand-spun turbans, while the folk arts section showcases traditional musical instruments including the Sarangi, Been, and Dholak. The museum stands as an indispensable cultural bridge, allowing younger generations and travelers to connect intimately with the authentic pastoral roots and communal warmth of rural Haryana.'
WHERE id = 48;

-- #49: Jahaj Kothi Museum (Hisar, Haryana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Jahaj Kothi Museum in Hisar, Haryana, is a fascinating historical landmark and archaeological repository maintained by the Department of Archaeology and Museums of the Government of Haryana. Originally an eighteenth-century structure resembling a ship surrounded by water—hence earning the local moniker ''Jahaj'' (ship) Kothi—it served as the fortified residence of the colorful Irish mercenary and adventurer George Thomas, who established an independent principality in Haryana in the late eighteenth century. Today, the heritage building houses an important regional archaeological museum displaying stone sculptures, coins, pottery, and inscriptions excavated from ancient nearby sites such as Agroha, Hansi, and Rakhigarhi. Notable exhibits include exquisitely carved medieval Jain and Hindu stone statues, architectural fragments, and weapons, offering visitors a unique perspective into both regional medieval history and eighteenth-century military adventurism.'
WHERE id = 49;

-- #50: State Museum, Shimla (Shimla, Himachal Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Himachal State Museum, perched atop the peaceful Chaura Maidan hill in the heritage capital of Shimla, is the foremost cultural institution dedicated to preserving the artistic and cultural heritage of the Western Himalayas. Housed in a charming colonial-era mansion that once served as the private residence of Lord William Beresford, the museum opened in 1974 with curated galleries across archaeology, painting, ethnology, and numismatics. The museum is internationally renowned for its collection of delicate Pahari miniature paintings from the Kangra, Basohli, and Mandi schools, illustrating classical poetry and Vaishnavite themes. Visitors can also admire intricately embroidered Chamba rumals, carved wooden temple panels, bronze and stone sculptures from ancient Himalayan shrines, traditional Himachali jewelry, folk musical instruments, and regional costumes, offering an authentic celebration of mountain cultural heritage.'
WHERE id = 50;

-- #51: Kangra Art Museum (Shimla, Himachal Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Kangra Art Museum, located in Dharamshala in the shadow of the Dhauladhar mountains in Himachal Pradesh, is an exceptional cultural institution dedicated to the preservation of the artistic legacy of the historic Kangra Valley. Established to honor the artistic traditions that flourished under the patronage of the Katoch royal dynasty, the museum is celebrated for its exquisite collection of Kangra miniature paintings. These delicate artworks, characterized by lyrical lines, naturalistic landscape detailing, and serene depictions of the divine love of Radha and Krishna, represent the pinnacle of Indian miniature art. The museum also displays royal court costumes, palanquins, ancient coins, traditional woodcarvings, metal sculptures, weapons, and pottery used in the Kangra region, providing visitors with a profound understanding of the royal sophistication and devotional art of the Western Himalayas.'
WHERE id = 51;

-- #52: SPS Museum (Srinagar, Jammu & Kashmir)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Sri Pratap Singh (SPS) Museum, established in 1898 on the banks of the Jhelum River in Lal Mandi, Srinagar, is the premier cultural institution of Jammu and Kashmir. Housed in a newly constructed modern facility, the museum holds an extraordinary collection of over seventy thousand historical artefacts illustrating Kashmir''s rich syncretic heritage. World-famous exhibits include the ancient moulded terracotta tiles from the second-century Buddhist site of Harwan, Gandharan sculptures, and masterfully sculpted Kashmiri bronze and stone idols of Shiva and Vishnu. The museum also houses an invaluable collection of handwoven Pashmina and Jamawar shawls, papier-mâché crafts, walnut wood carvings, rare Persian and Sanskrit manuscripts (including fifth-century Gilgit birch-bark scriptures), royal weaponry, and historic coins, offering a comprehensive narrative of the Valley''s profound intellectual and artistic traditions.'
WHERE id = 52;

-- #53: Stok Palace Museum (Srinagar, Jammu & Kashmir)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Stok Palace Museum, situated within the historic nineteenth-century Stok Palace near Leh in Ladakh (historically administered with Jammu and Kashmir), is the living heritage residence of the royal Namgyal dynasty of Ladakh. Built around 1820 by King Tsespal Tondup Namgyal, the palace commands sweeping views of the Indus Valley and the snowcapped Stok Kangri range. The palace museum houses an extraordinary royal collection of Tibetan Buddhist art, religious relics, and dynastic heirlooms. Highlights include the Queen''s ceremonial headdress (Perak), encrusted with hundreds of natural turquoise stones, coral, and gold, alongside antique silk thangkas dating back to the sixteenth century. Visitors can also view royal crowns, prayer wheels, gem-studded jewellery, ceremonial swords, coins, and vintage porcelain, making it an indispensable destination for exploring the spiritual and regal traditions of high-altitude Himalayan culture.'
WHERE id = 53;

-- #54: State Museum Hotwar (Ranchi, Jharkhand)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The State Museum Hotwar, also known as the Jharkhand State Museum, is located in the Khelgaon complex at Hotwar in Ranchi. Established to document and celebrate the profound natural and human history of the Chota Nagpur plateau, this modern institution features sixteen comprehensive galleries. The archaeological section displays prehistoric stone implements from the Palaeolithic and Mesolithic eras, ancient terracotta figures, and medieval stone sculptures excavated from heritage sites across the state. The museum''s ethnographic wing is particularly renowned, offering authentic representations of the distinctive lifestyle, music, festivals, and architectural traditions of Jharkhand''s thirty-two indigenous tribes, including the Santhal, Munda, Oraon, and Ho. With dedicated galleries of arms, numismatics, folk crafts, and contemporary art, the museum serves as the premier cultural showcase for Jharkhand''s diverse heritage.'
WHERE id = 54;

-- #55: Tribal Research Institute Museum (Ranchi, Jharkhand)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Tribal Research Institute Museum, located on Morabadi Road in Ranchi, Jharkhand, is an authoritative anthropological museum managed by the Dr. Ram Dayal Munda Tribal Welfare Research Institute. Established to advance the scholarly study and preservation of indigenous culture, the museum offers visitors an authentic, detailed perspective on the socio-cultural fabric of the region''s tribal communities. The exhibits display a vast array of tribal material culture, including hand-carved agricultural implements, traditional hunting arrows and bows, indigenous grain-storage containers, musical instruments like the Madar and Nagada, and ritual totems. Lifelike dioramas recreate traditional tribal huts with authentic wall murals and domestic furnishings. Through educational documentation of tribal folklore, customary laws, and artisanal techniques, the museum plays a crucial role in safeguarding the rich living heritage of Jharkhand''s indigenous peoples.'
WHERE id = 55;

-- #56: Mysore Sand Sculpture Museum (Bengaluru, Karnataka)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mysore Sand Sculpture Museum, located at the foothills of Chamundi Hill along the road from Mysuru, is India''s first dedicated sand sculpture museum. Conceptualized and created by talented woman sand artist M. N. Gowri, the museum spans an open-sided, weatherproof canopy that protects over one hundred and fifty intricate sand sculptures. Using over one hundred truckloads of construction sand combined solely with water and eco-friendly binding agents, the artist has sculpted remarkable thematic compositions. Visitors can admire finely detailed sculptures depicting the famous Mysuru Dasara royal procession, wildlife scenes, ancient Egyptian pharaohs, Lord Ganesha, mythological episodes from the Mahabharata, and popular fairytale figures. Recognized for its unique artistry and technical innovation, this privately maintained cultural venue provides a delightful artistic stop for travelers exploring the heritage belt of southern Karnataka.'
WHERE id = 56;

-- #57: Railway Museum (Bengaluru, Karnataka)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Railway Museum in Mysuru, established in 1979 by Indian Railways, is the second railway museum set up in India after the National Rail Museum in New Delhi. Located near the Mysuru railway station, this outdoor heritage museum preserves the captivating history and technological evolution of the railways in South India. The centerpiece attraction is the Maharani''s Saloon, a luxurious royal carriage built in 1899 for the Queen of Mysore, complete with original dining salons, ceiling fans, and vintage wooden furnishings. Visitors can inspect an array of historic steam locomotives, an Austin rail-motor car, wooden inspection coaches, antique signaling equipment, and mechanical weighbridges. A mini toy train operates around the landscaped grounds, making the museum an immensely popular family destination celebrating India''s extraordinary railway legacy.'
WHERE id = 57;

-- #58: Mysore Palace Residential Museum (Mysuru, Karnataka)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mysore Palace Residential Museum, situated within the majestic Amba Vilas Palace complex in Mysuru, offers an intimate look into the regal lifestyle of the Wadiyar dynasty, the historical rulers of the Kingdom of Mysore. While the main palace halls display state ceremonial splendour, the Residential Museum preserves the personal living quarters, private collections, and royal heirlooms of the Maharajas. The exhibits include exquisite silver and teak furniture, crystal chandeliers, royal palanquins, ceremonial howdahs, and antique European and Indian clocks. The armoury showcases swords, daggers, and matchlocks used by royal guards, while the portrait gallery displays fine oil paintings depicting royal coronations, Dasara processions, and court gatherings. Preserving the refined artistic patronage and sovereign dignity of the Wadiyars, the museum remains one of Karnataka''s most visited royal heritage sites.'
WHERE id = 58;

-- #59: Hill Palace Museum (Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Hill Palace Museum, situated at Tripunithura near Kochi in Kerala, is the largest archaeological museum in Kerala and the former administrative seat of the Cochin royal family. Built in 1865, the palace complex spans fifty-four sprawling acres and comprises forty-nine buildings showcasing traditional Kerala architectural style alongside European influences, surrounded by terraced gardens and a deer park. The museum features fourteen curated galleries displaying royal treasures of the Cochin Maharajas. Its crowning glory is the royal gold crown, intricately studded with ninety-five diamonds and two thousand precious stones. Visitors can also admire royal ceremonial palanquins, silver throne chairs, Chinese porcelain, medieval weapons, prehistoric megalithic stone sarcophagi, and historical temple mural paintings. The museum provides an unforgettable window into Kerala''s monarchical past and archaeological riches.'
WHERE id = 59;

-- #60: Kerela State Museum (Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Kerala State Museum, popularly known as the Napier Museum, is an iconic landmark located in the heart of Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala. Established in 1855 and named after the Governor of Madras Lord Napier, the current building, completed in 1880, is an architectural masterpiece designed by Robert Chisholm. It features a unique Indo-Saracenic design combining Gothic gables and minarets with indigenous Kerala woodcraft and natural cooling systems. The museum preserves an exceptional collection of South Indian archaeological artefacts, including ancient bronze idols of Shiva, Parvati, and Vishnu dating from the eighth to eighteenth centuries. Other displays include intricately carved ivory tusks, traditional wooden temple chariots, Kathakali dance costumes, antique jewelry, and historical coins. Surrounded by lush botanical gardens, the museum represents the zenith of nineteenth-century regional museum architecture.'
WHERE id = 60;

-- #61: Indo-Portuguese Museums (Kochi, Kerala)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Indo-Portuguese Museum, situated in the tranquil gardens of the Bishop''s House in Fort Kochi, Kerala, was established through the tireless efforts of the late Bishop Joseph Kureethara to preserve the cultural and religious heritage of the Portuguese in India. Housed in a thoughtfully designed modern building, the museum showcases the unique artistic fusion that emerged when Portuguese Catholic traditions blended with indigenous Indian artisanal craftsmanship. The collection is divided into five thematic wings: Altar, Treasure, Procession, Civil Life, and Cathedral. Notable exhibits include a sixteenth-century teakwood altarpiece, silver processional crosses, vestments woven with gold and silver thread, monstrances, and carved ivory crucifixes. The museum provides visitors with an illuminating journey into the historic maritime, religious, and artistic dialogue between Portugal and the ancient spice coast of Malabar.'
WHERE id = 61;

-- #62: Mattancherry Palace and Museum (Kochi, Kerala)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mattancherry Palace, popularly known as the Dutch Palace, is a sixteenth-century royal landmark located in Mattancherry, Kochi. Built around 1555 by the Portuguese as a gift to Raja Veera Kerala Varma and subsequently renovated by the Dutch, the palace is renowned for its traditional Nalukettu architectural layout featuring timber ceilings and a central courtyard. The palace museum is world-famous for its breathtaking temple murals that adorn the royal bedchambers, depicting scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and Krishna Lila executed with extraordinary stylistic vigor and natural pigments. The Coronation Hall showcases life-sized portraits of the Cochin Maharajas from 1864 onwards, ceremonial palanquins, royal umbrellas, ivory palanquin fittings, and historic Dutch maps. Maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India, the palace stands as a peerless jewel of Kerala''s royal art.'
WHERE id = 62;

-- #63: State Museum Bhopal (Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The State Museum Bhopal, situated atop the scenic Shyamla Hills overlooking the city in Madhya Pradesh, is a state-of-the-art museum facility housing an encyclopedic collection of central Indian antiquities. Inaugurated in 2005, the museum features sixteen modern, themed galleries organized with international standards of lighting and display. Highlights include an extraordinary collection of ancient and medieval stone sculptures excavated from Vidisha, Khajuraho, and Hinglajgarh, spanning the Maurya, Sunga, Gupta, and Chandela periods. A globally renowned section displays rare Jain bronzes recovered from Dhar, reflecting supreme metallurgical skill. Visitors can also explore galleries dedicated to the royal carriages of the Begums of Bhopal, miniature paintings, numismatics, rare manuscripts, antique weapons, and postage stamps. The museum stands as an essential cultural destination chronicling the rich civilizational legacy of Madhya Pradesh.'
WHERE id = 63;

-- #64: Tribal Museum Bhopal (Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Madhya Pradesh Tribal Museum, located on Shyamla Hills in Bhopal, is celebrated as one of the most innovative and visually breathtaking experiential museums in India. Inaugurated in 2013, the museum is uniquely designed to showcase the vibrant mythologies, spiritual cosmos, and daily living environments of the state''s major indigenous tribes, including the Gond, Bhil, Baiga, Korku, Sahariya, and Kol. Instead of displaying artefacts behind glass cases, the museum features massive, life-sized artistic installations crafted entirely by authentic tribal artisans. Visitors wander through full-scale traditional houses, mystical sacred groves, wedding mandaps, and cosmic representations filled with terracotta bells, wooden totems, and colorful tribal paintings. By celebrating the creative living wisdom and ecological worldview of tribal communities, the museum provides an unforgettable, immersive cultural experience.'
WHERE id = 64;

-- #65: Archaeological Museum Khajuraho (Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Archaeological Museum Khajuraho, maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India near the world-famous Western Group of Temples in Khajuraho, preserves the finest loose sculptures retrieved from the UNESCO World Heritage temple complex. Originally founded by British engineer W. E. Jardine in 1910 and later relocated to its present modern facility, the museum holds over two thousand masterpieces of tenth-to-twelfth-century Chandela art. Across five spacious galleries, visitors can view exquisite stone carvings of Hindu and Jain deities, celestial nymphs (Apsaras), mythical beasts (Vyalas), and daily medieval court scenes. The museum''s centerpiece is a colossal, magnificent four-headed stone sculpture of Vaikuntha Vishnu, alongside celebrated carvings of dancing Ganesha and Mahavira. The museum provides an essential complement to exploring the architectural majesty and sculptural perfection of Khajuraho.'
WHERE id = 65;

-- #66: Don Bosco Museum (Shillong, Meghalaya)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Don Bosco Museum of Indigenous Cultures, situated within the Sacred Heart Theological College campus at Mawlai in Shillong, Meghalaya, is widely acclaimed as the finest and most comprehensive ethnographic museum in Northeast India. Rising across seven striking hexagonal storeys, the museum houses seventeen curated galleries that provide an in-depth exploration of the diverse cultures, traditions, and arts of all eight North Eastern states. Visitors can explore galleries dedicated to tribal attire, musical instruments, agricultural techniques, traditional dwelling architecture, weaponry, and basketry. Specialized wings showcase photographic history, religious traditions, and regional flora and fauna. The museum''s rooftop skywalk offers breathtaking panoramic views over the pine-covered hills of Shillong. Through scholarly curation and immersive displays, the museum fosters national appreciation for the vibrant cultural diversity of Northeast India.'
WHERE id = 66;

-- #67: Wankhar Entomology Museum (Shillong, Meghalaya)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Wankhar Entomology Museum, located in the Riatsamthiah neighborhood of Shillong, Meghalaya, is a rare, privately owned natural history museum founded in the 1930s by entomologist S. J. Wankhar. Dedicated to the study and appreciation of insects, this specialized museum preserves an astonishing collection of thousands of mounted butterflies, moths, rhinoceros beetles, and stick insects collected from the biodiversity hotspots of Northeast India and across the globe. Visitors can admire striking specimens including the colossal Atlas Moth (one of the largest insects in the world), iridescent Swallowtails, and masterfully camouflaged Leaf Insects. The museum promotes environmental education by emphasizing the vital ecological role insects play as pollinators and indicators of forest health. This charming boutique museum offers an intriguing and educational stop for nature enthusiasts visiting the Scotland of the East.'
WHERE id = 67;

-- #68: Mizoram State Museum (Aizawl, Mizoram)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mizoram State Museum, located atop Macdonald Hill in the scenic capital city of Aizawl, is the premier cultural repository documenting the distinctive heritage and social evolution of the Mizo people. Established in 1977, the museum features five curated galleries covering ethnology, archaeology, natural history, and historical photographs. The ethnographic collection offers an authentic view into traditional Mizo village life prior to modernization. Highlights include traditional handwoven Mizo shawls (Puan) featuring distinctive clan motifs, intricate cane and bamboo basketry, brass gongs, traditional hunting implements, and smoked tobacco pipes. A life-sized reconstruction of a traditional bachelor''s dormitory, or ''Zawlbuk'', demonstrates the ancient community educational system that instilled virtues of courage and mutual service (Tlawmngaihna). The museum remains an essential cultural destination for discovering the living traditions of Mizoram.'
WHERE id = 68;

-- #69: State Museum Kohima (Kohima, Nagaland)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Nagaland State Museum, situated on Bayavü Hill in Kohima, is the foremost cultural institution dedicated to preserving the vibrant material heritage, rituals, and customs of the sixteen recognized Naga tribes. Across two spacious floors, the museum exhibits an authentic and comprehensive collection of traditional Naga tribal attire, warrior shawls with distinctive clan patterns, shell necklaces, brass neckbands, and warrior headgear decorated with wild boar tusks and hornbill feathers. The central courtyard features life-sized authentic replicas of traditional Naga Morungs (youth dormitories), carved with totemic animal figures representing strength and fertility. Visitors can also explore ancient ceremonial monolith stones, wooden musical log drums, agricultural tools, and archaeological finds from across the state, making the museum an indispensable venue for experiencing the rich cultural identity of Nagaland.'
WHERE id = 69;

-- #70: World War II Museum (Kohima, Nagaland)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The World War II Museum, located within the Kisama Heritage Village near Kohima, Nagaland, commemorates the pivotal Battle of Kohima fought in 1944. Historically regarded by military historians as the ''Stalingrad of the East'', the battle marked the turning point where Allied forces halted the invasion of British India by the Imperial Japanese Army. The museum preserves a poignant collection of battlefield relics unearthed from the surrounding ridges, including artillery pieces, machine guns, mortar shells, personal field gear, steel helmets, and military radios used by both Allied and Japanese soldiers. Visitors can inspect archival photographs, campaign maps, wartime uniforms, personal letters, and newspaper clippings that illustrate the harrowing conditions of jungle warfare and the invaluable contributions of local Naga scouts. The museum serves as an evocative monument to peace and remembrance.'
WHERE id = 70;

-- #71: State Museum Bhubaneswar (Bhubaneswar, Odisha)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Odisha State Museum, established in 1932 on Lewis Road in Bhubaneswar, is the premier historical and cultural institution of Odisha. Across numerous specialized galleries, the museum presents a panoramic overview of the state''s deep maritime, artistic, and dynastic history. It is globally celebrated for holding one of the world''s largest collections of ancient palm-leaf manuscripts, including beautifully illustrated seventeenth-century manuscripts of the Gitagovinda. The archaeological galleries display ancient stone sculptures from the Buddhist diamond triangle sites of Ratnagiri and Lalitagiri, alongside Jain statues from Khandagiri and temple sculptures of the Somavamsi and Ganga dynasties. Other galleries showcase prehistoric stone tools, ancient copper-plate charters, medieval arms, tribal crafts, and natural history specimens, making it an essential institution for understanding Odisha''s profound civilizational legacy.'
WHERE id = 71;

-- #72: Tribal Museum (Bhubaneswar, Odisha)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Tribal Museum in Bhubaneswar, formally known as the Museum of Tribal Arts and Artefacts and locally celebrated as the ''Tribal Habitat'', is a premier anthropological museum under the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes Research and Training Institute. Recognized as one of the finest tribal museums in India, it showcases the living cultures of Odisha''s sixty-two indigenous tribes, including thirteen particularly vulnerable groups like the Bonda, Dongria Kondh, and Saora. The museum grounds feature authentic, full-scale replicas of traditional tribal dwellings built with indigenous materials, complete with symbolic Saora wall murals (Idital). The indoor galleries display intricate Dokra bell-metal figurines, beaded jewelry, woven shawls, musical instruments, agricultural tools, and hunting implements, offering visitors a deeply respectful appreciation for the sustainable wisdom and cultural richness of Odisha''s tribal communities.'
WHERE id = 72;

-- #73: Odisha State Maritime Museum (Cuttack, Odisha)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Odisha State Maritime Museum, situated on the scenic banks of the Mahanadi River at Jobra in Cuttack, is a landmark museum commemorating the ancient maritime supremacy of the historical kingdom of Kalinga. Housed within the meticulously restored nineteenth-century maritime workshop built by British engineers in 1869, the museum spans thirteen expansive galleries. The exhibits narrate the heroic journeys of ancient Sadhavas (Kalinga merchants) who sailed merchant vessels across the Indian Ocean to trade with Bali, Java, Sumatra, and Sri Lanka, a heritage celebrated annually during the historic Bali Yatra festival. Visitors can explore traditional boatbuilding yards, navigational equipment, marine propulsion engines, full-scale wooden boat replicas, historical maritime maps, and modern shipbuilding displays, providing an inspiring tribute to Odisha''s centuries of oceanic enterprise.'
WHERE id = 73;

-- #74: Government Museum Chandigarh (Amritsar, Punjab)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Government Museum and Art Gallery in Chandigarh (historically connected with the cultural collections of Punjab) is one of India''s premier museums of fine art and archaeology. Established in 1968, the museum building is an architectural masterpiece designed by the renowned Swiss-French architect Le Corbusier. The museum owes its exceptional collection to the 1947 Partition of India, when the antiquities of the Central Museum in Lahore were divided, giving Chandigarh one of the world''s most renowned collections of Gandharan Buddhist stone sculptures dating from the first to fourth centuries CE. The museum is equally celebrated for its extraordinary repository of Pahari and Rajasthani miniature paintings, medieval Indian stone carvings, ancient coins, and seminal modern Indian paintings by artists such as Amrita Sher-Gil, M. F. Husain, and Jamini Roy, offering a world-class aesthetic journey.'
WHERE id = 74;

-- #75: Partition Museum Amritsar (Amritsar, Punjab)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Partition Museum, located in the historic Town Hall of Amritsar near the Golden Temple, is the world''s first museum dedicated entirely to the memory of the 1947 Partition of British India. Conceptualized by The Arts and Cultural Heritage Trust, the museum provides a profoundly moving, people-centric narrative of the largest mass migration in human history. Through fourteen interactive galleries, the museum tells the stories of millions who lost their lives or were displaced through personal oral history recordings, letters, family photographs, and refugee memorabilia such as antique trunks, bridal shawls, and sewing machines carried across borders. The galleries document the freedom struggle, the boundary commission, the harrowing migration journeys, and the resilient rebuilding of lives in refugee camps, culminating in the serene ''Gallery of Hope'' designed to inspire peace and reconciliation.'
WHERE id = 75;

-- #76: Maharaja Ranjit Singh Museum (Amritsar, Punjab)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Maharaja Ranjit Singh Museum, situated in the historic Ram Bagh heritage gardens of Amritsar, is dedicated to the life, military campaigns, and sovereign reign of the ''Lion of Punjab'', Maharaja Ranjit Singh. Housed within his historic summer palace constructed in 1818, the museum captures the grandeur of the sovereign Sikh Empire. Across its stately halls, the museum displays an impressive collection of military artefacts, including the armour, chain-mail, shields, and matchlocks used by soldiers of the Khalsa army. Visitors can admire finely detailed miniature paintings portraying royal durbars and battle scenes, royal decrees (parwanas), manuscripts, and rare coins minted during the Maharaja''s reign. With scenic gardens, historic palace architecture, and educational displays on nineteenth-century Punjabi chivalry, the museum provides an inspiring tribute to the heroic legacy of the Sikh Empire.'
WHERE id = 76;

-- #77: Mehrangarh Museum (Jaipur, Rajasthan)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Mehrangarh Museum, situated within the colossal fifteenth-century Mehrangarh Fort rising four hundred feet above the city of Jodhpur in Rajasthan, is widely recognized as one of the finest fort museums in the world. Maintained by the Mehrangarh Museum Trust, the museum showcases the royal splendors of the Rathore dynasty across immaculately preserved palace chambers. Highlights include the Sheesh Mahal (Hall of Mirrors), the opulent Phool Mahal (Flower Palace), and the Daulat Khana gallery. The museum holds priceless royal palanquins (including the gold-embossed Mahadol palanquin), intricately carved royal howdahs (elephant seats), miniature paintings from the Marwar school, ceremonial carpets, musical instruments, and one of India''s finest collections of historic armour and swords, offering an unforgettable encounter with Rajput chivalry and royal artistry.'
WHERE id = 77;

-- #78: Namgyal Institute of Tibetology (Gangtok, Sikkim)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Namgyal Institute of Tibetology, located amidst serene forests in Deorali, Gangtok, is an internationally renowned autonomous research institute and museum dedicated to the study of Tibetan culture, language, and Mahayana Buddhism. Established in 1958, with its foundation stone laid by the fourteenth Dalai Lama, the institute is housed in an impressive Tibetan-style mansion featuring traditional woodcarvings and frescoed prayer walls. The museum contains an invaluable collection of sacred Buddhist relics, including eleventh-century Sanskrit manuscripts on palm leaf and silk, silver thangkas, consecrated statues of Gautama Buddha and Padmasambhava, and ritual musical instruments made of conch shells and brass. The museum also preserves rare Lepcha cultural artefacts and historical coins, serving as a revered spiritual and academic beacon for Himalayan Buddhist scholarship.'
WHERE id = 78;

-- #79: Government Museum (Chennai, Tamil Nadu)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Government Museum system of Tamil Nadu, celebrated through its monumental state collections, preserves an unparalleled legacy of South Indian art, archaeology, and natural sciences. The museum''s archaeological holdings encompass magnificent stone sculptures from the Pallava, Chola, and Pandya dynasties, reflecting the evolution of Dravidian temple art from rock-cut monoliths to finely contoured granite deities. The world-renowned bronze collections display iconic masterworks of the Chola golden age, including cosmic depictions of Nataraja, Parvati, and Shaivite saints. Visitors can also explore extensive numismatic cabinets holding punch-marked coins, Roman denarii from coastal trade, prehistoric megalithic burial urns, and ethnological collections representing tribal communities like the Todas of the Nilgiris. The museum provides an essential, scholarly destination for appreciating Tamil Nadu''s peerless civilizational accomplishments.'
WHERE id = 79;

-- #80: Salar Jung Museum (Hyderabad, Telangana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Salar Jung Museum, situated on the southern bank of the Musi River in Hyderabad, is one of the three National Museums of India and one of the largest private art collections in the world. Assembled primarily by Nawab Mir Yousuf Ali Khan (Salar Jung III), former Prime Minister of the seventh Nizam of Hyderabad, the museum contains over forty-three thousand art objects, manuscripts, and rare books from across the globe. Among its celebrated masterpieces are the breathtaking Italian marble statue ''Veiled Rebecca'' sculpted by Giovanni Maria Benzoni in 1876, the nineteenth-century British bracket musical clock with a mechanical toy figure, and Tipu Sultan''s personal wardrobe and ivory chairs. Galleries showcase Mughal jade daggers, Chinese celadon porcelain, Japanese lacquer ware, Persian carpets, and European oil paintings, presenting a monument to passionate cultural connoisseurship.'
WHERE id = 80;

-- #81: State Archaeological Museum (Hyderabad, Telangana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Telangana State Archaeology Museum, located in the Public Gardens in Nampally, Hyderabad, is the oldest museum in the state. Established in 1920 by the seventh Nizam, Mir Osman Ali Khan, under the curatorship of renowned archaeologist Ghulam Yazdani, the museum is housed in an imposing Indo-Saracenic building. The museum is globally celebrated for housing an authentic ancient Egyptian mummy of Princess Naishu, dating to around 2500 BCE, acquired by the Nizam. Other highlights include an extraordinary collection of Kakatiya and Ikshvaku stone sculptures, early Buddhist limestone reliefs from Nelakondapally, Jain bronzes, medieval arms, and copper-plate charters. The Ajanta Pavilion displays faithful full-scale reproduction copies of the celebrated Ajanta cave frescoes painted in the 1920s, making it an indispensable destination for exploring the Deccan''s deep civilizational past.'
WHERE id = 81;

-- #82: City Museum (Hyderabad, Telangana)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The City Museum of Hyderabad, located within the historic Purani Haveli palace complex, was inaugurated in 2012 to celebrate the four-century-old urban, cultural, and political history of Hyderabad. Established alongside the Nizam''s Silver Jubilee Museum, the City Museum presents a chronological narrative tracing the city''s journey from prehistoric times through the Golconda Sultanate to the Asaf Jahi era and modern times. Exhibits display Neolithic stone implements and pottery excavated around the Musi river basin, medieval coins, ancient maps of the walled city, European traveler accounts, and historical photographs documenting civic infrastructure, railways, and historic flooding. Visitors can examine royal gifts, textiles, and everyday artefacts reflecting the cosmopolitan Ganga-Jamuni tehzeeb of Hyderabad, making it a captivating historical showcase for residents and visitors alike.'
WHERE id = 82;

-- #83: Tripura State Museum (Agartala, Tripura)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Tripura State Museum is housed in the magnificent Ujjayanta Palace, a gleaming white Neoclassical royal residence built in 1901 by Maharaja Radha Kishore Manikya in the heart of Agartala. Once the seat of the Manikya kings of Tripura, the palatial complex features grand tiled courtyards, Mughal-style formal gardens, musical fountains, and classical domes. Transformed into the premier state museum, it houses twenty-two curated galleries dedicated to the art, archaeology, and vibrant tribal culture of Tripura and Northeast India. Key exhibits include ancient Buddhist and Hindu stone sculptures excavated from the monumental rock-carving site of Pilak, royal thrones, dynastic coins, traditional weapons, and intricate handwoven textiles representing the state''s nineteen indigenous tribes. The museum provides an inspiring tribute to the regal dignity and multicultural heritage of Tripura.'
WHERE id = 83;

-- #84: State Museum Lucknow (Agra, Uttar Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The State Museum Lucknow (historically representing Uttar Pradesh''s foundational state archaeology collections) is the oldest regional museum in Uttar Pradesh, established in 1863. Located in the lush environs of Banarasi Bagh in Lucknow, this grand multidisciplinary institution holds over one hundred thousand antiquities spanning five millennia. The museum is internationally celebrated for its archaeological collections from Mathura, featuring priceless Kushana and Gupta stone sculptures, early Buddhist and Jain Ayagapatas, and ancient terracottas. A dedicated Egyptian gallery houses a rare, authentic three-thousand-year-old Egyptian mummy. The Awadh cultural wing displays royal textiles, silver huqqas, miniature paintings, and historical weaponry of the Nawabs, alongside extensive numismatic collections containing ancient punch-marked coins, Gupta gold dinars, and medieval coinage, offering an authoritative exploration of North India''s artistic evolution.'
WHERE id = 84;

-- #85: Sarnath Museum (Agra, Uttar Pradesh)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Archaeological Site Museum at Sarnath, situated near Varanasi in Uttar Pradesh, is the oldest site museum of the Archaeological Survey of India, completed in 1910. Located directly adjacent to the sacred deer park where Gautama Buddha preached his first sermon after attaining enlightenment, the museum houses the priceless antiquities excavated from this hallowed ground. The undisputed crown jewel of the collection is the world-famous Lion Capital of Ashoka (third century BCE), sculpted from polished Chunar sandstone, which was adopted as the official National Emblem of the Republic of India. Another world-famous masterpiece is the fifth-century Gupta stone sculpture of the Preaching Buddha (Dharmachakra Pravartana). With galleries containing hundreds of Buddhist images, inscribed votive stupas, and architectural fragments, the museum stands as a monumental sanctuary of ancient Indian art.'
WHERE id = 85;

-- #86: State Museum Dehradun (Dehradun, Uttarakhand)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The State Museum Dehradun, located in the scenic Doon Valley of Uttarakhand, is a dedicated cultural institution established to preserve and celebrate the rich artistic, archaeological, and ethnographic heritage of the Central Himalayas. The museum presents curated exhibits that illustrate the cultural traditions of the Garhwal and Kumaon regions. Visitors can view ancient temple stone sculptures, medieval copper-plate inscriptions, coins of historical regional dynasties, and traditional weaponry used by regional warriors. The ethnographic wing showcases the distinctive lifestyle and material culture of indigenous communities such as the Jaunsari, Bhotia, and Tharu tribes, featuring traditional hill attire, silver jewelry, hand-carved wooden domestic doors, and musical instruments. The museum offers visitors an enlightening orientation into the sacred history, folklore, and living traditions of the Devbhoomi region.'
WHERE id = 86;

-- #87: Jim Corbett Museum (Dehradun, Uttarakhand)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Jim Corbett Museum, situated at Kaladhungi in Nainital district in the foothills of Uttarakhand, is housed in the charming heritage stone cottage that once served as the winter home of legendary naturalist, hunter, and author Jim Corbett. Surrounded by verdant orchards and native flora, the cottage has been carefully converted into a memorial museum celebrating Corbett''s life and his pioneering role in Indian wildlife conservation. The exhibits display his personal letters, handwritten manuscripts, antique furniture, historical photographs, fishing equipment, and original editions of his bestselling books, including ''Man-Eaters of Kumaon''. Educational displays chronicle how his profound empathy for local villagers and wildlife led to the creation of Hailey National Park, now Corbett National Park. The museum provides an evocative, inspiring tribute to the birth of modern conservation in India.'
WHERE id = 87;

-- #88: Hazarduari Palace Museum (Murshidabad, West Bengal)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Hazarduari Palace Museum, located along the eastern bank of the Bhagirathi River in Murshidabad, West Bengal, is a magnificent nineteenth-century palace built between 1829 and 1837 for Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah of Bengal. Designed in the Greek Doric architectural style by British engineer Duncan MacLeod, the palace derives its name from its one thousand doors, of which nine hundred are real and one hundred are false, built as a security measure against intruders. Maintained by the Archaeological Survey of India, the palace museum houses an extraordinary royal collection of the Nawabs of Bengal. Across twenty galleries, visitors can view royal silver thrones, vintage carriages, Belgian crystal chandeliers, swords and armaments used by Siraj-ud-Daulah, miniature paintings, and a library containing rare illuminated Quranic manuscripts and treaty documents.'
WHERE id = 88;

-- #89: Victoria Memorial (Kolkata, West Bengal)
UPDATE museums
SET description = 'The Victoria Memorial, standing gracefully amidst sixty-four acres of manicured gardens on the Maidan in Kolkata, is an iconic national museum and one of the finest colonial monuments in India. Conceived by Lord Curzon to commemorate Queen Victoria and opened to the public in 1921, the grand edifice is constructed from pristine white Makrana marble in the Indo-Saracenic Revival style, crowned by a rotating bronze Angel of Victory. The museum houses twenty-five galleries displaying over thirty thousand historic artefacts. Highlights include the world''s largest collection of paintings by Thomas and William Daniell, royal British and Indian portraits, rare Mughal miniatures, and historic arms and armour. The dedicated Kolkata Gallery provides a fascinating visual narrative of the city''s architectural, cultural, and political evolution from a trading settlement into the imperial capital of British India.'
WHERE id = 89;

